.TH MEMTOP 1 "August 2026" "memtop" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
memtop \- show Linux processes by resident memory usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memtop
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B memtop
is a Bash and awk command-line process memory inspector for Linux.
It reads process data from GNU
.BR ps (1),
orders rows by resident set size or memory percentage, applies optional
filters, and renders a table, JSON document, or CSV stream.
.PP
The default invocation shows 20 processes ordered from highest to lowest RSS.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Selection and sorting
.TP
.BR \-n " " N ", " \-\-num " " N ", " \-\-num= N
Show at most
.I N
rows. The default is 20.
.TP
.BR \-s " " FIELD ", " \-\-sort " " FIELD ", " \-\-sort= FIELD
Sort by
.B rss
or
.BR pmem .
The default is
.BR rss .
.TP
.BR \-u " " UNIT ", " \-\-unit " " UNIT ", " \-\-unit= UNIT
Display RSS with
.BR auto ,
.BR kb ,
.BR mb ,
or
.BR gb .
.TP
.BR \-\-user " " USER ", " \-\-user= USER
Show only processes owned by
.IR USER .
.TP
.BR \-\-pid " " PID ", " \-\-pid= PID
Show only the specified process ID.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit-rss " " SIZE ", " \-\-limit-rss= SIZE
Show only processes whose RSS is at least
.IR SIZE .
Accepted suffixes are K, M, and G. A suffix-free value is interpreted as KiB.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the selected sort order.
.SS Presentation
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full
Show the full command line instead of the command name.
.TP
.B \-\-no-header
Do not print the table or CSV header.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Disable ANSI color and suppress the header.
.TP
.BR \-\-self ", " \-\-hide-self
Hide memtop, ps, and awk helper processes from the result.
The
.B \-\-self
name is retained as a compatibility alias.
.TP
.B \-\-tree
Add PPID and light process-tree indentation.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " N"
Trim displayed command text to at most
.I N
characters.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Add displayed RSS and memory-percentage totals to table or CSV output.
JSON output already contains aggregate fields.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI color.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Force ANSI color.
.SS Output and repeated collection
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write a JSON document.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Write CSV output.
.TP
.BR \-\-log " " FILE ", " \-\-log= FILE
Append each rendered snapshot to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BR \-w " " SEC ", " \-\-watch " " SEC ", " \-\-watch= SEC
Refresh every
.I SEC
seconds. Decimal intervals such as 0.5 are accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Run one collection even when watch mode is supplied.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show command help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.SH SIZE VALUES
Examples of valid minimum RSS values:
.PP
.nf
500M
1.5G
250000K
250000
.fi
.PP
K, M, and G are converted using powers of 1024.
.SH OUTPUT
Table output may contain PID, PPID, percent memory, formatted RSS, user, and
command columns depending on the selected options.
.PP
JSON output contains a generated timestamp, selected sort and unit, a process
array, displayed row count, total RSS, and total memory percentage.
.PP
CSV output uses this header by default:
.PP
.nf
pid,ppid,pmem,rss_kb,rss,user,command
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the default process list:
.PP
.nf
memtop
.fi
.PP
Show ten processes with full command lines:
.PP
.nf
memtop --num 10 --full
.fi
.PP
Show processes using at least 500 MiB of RSS:
.PP
.nf
memtop --limit-rss 500M --summary
.fi
.PP
Inspect one process:
.PP
.nf
memtop --pid 1928 --full --tree
.fi
.PP
Produce and validate JSON:
.PP
.nf
memtop --json --num 5 | python3 -m json.tool
.fi
.PP
Save CSV:
.PP
.nf
memtop --csv --num 100 > memtop.csv
.fi
.PP
Refresh every two seconds:
.PP
.nf
memtop --watch 2 --hide-self
.fi
.PP
Append snapshots to a log:
.PP
.nf
memtop --watch 5 --log "$HOME/memtop.log"
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/bin/memtop
Typical user-local executable path.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/share/man/man1/memtop.1
Typical user-local manual page path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A nonzero status indicates invalid input or a runtime
failure from memtop or a command in its process collection pipeline.
.SH SECURITY
Full process command lines can contain tokens, passwords, file paths, or other
sensitive arguments. Review output before sharing logs or reports.
.SH LIMITATIONS
RSS is not a direct measure of unique physical memory because shared pages may
be counted in more than one process. Tree indentation is a lightweight view
constructed from PPID data and is not a replacement for a dedicated process
tree inspector.
